  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "Ginn shut down St. Louis over six scoreless innings, and the offense found enough in the middle frames to hold on.",
    "what_this_means": "They trailed after four, then broke through in the fifth with four runs. Just enough cushion to finish with six. That's not a blowout; it's a controlled win built on clean pitching and timely contact. Thirteen hits meant the lineup was engaged, even if they left runners on in the early innings. Ginn gave them air to work with. Nick Kurtz converted when it mattered, driving in four runs. This is what a balanced game looks like for Oakland right now: a starter who doesn't leak, a lineup that finds its moment, and a bullpen that doesn't undo it. Luis Medina and Jack Perkins closed without incident, holding a one-run lead tight. The division is watching these margins. One-run wins stack differently than blowouts.",
    "what_to_watch": "Michael McGreevy takes the ball for St. Louis tonight with a 2.18 ERA. A different challenge than the Cardinals lineup Oakland just faced. That's not a cushion, that's a test.",
